  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 235 AM EST Fri Nov 13 2026\n\nToday-Sunday...Dry and chilly. A large and complex low pressure \nsystem over and offshore the northeastern US associated with a \nsubstantial mid-upper level low will sweep a couple more fronts \nthrough Florida early this morning and again later tonight, \ndelivering dry and actually cold air. Temperatures this morning \nare forecast to start in the M50s-M60s, then only warming to the \n70s this afternoon. Winds shift from NW-WNW-NNW between the two \nfronts, increasing to 10-15 mph with gusts to around 20 mph from \nthe late morning to sunset. Tonight into Saturday morning, \ntemperatures plunge into the M40s-M50s, the coldest of this snap. \nOvernight winds are forecast to generally remain 5-10 mph, which \nwill offset radiational cooling by keeping the boundary layer \nmixed, but cause wind chills to dip into the L-M40s for all but \nthe Space and Treasure Coasts, where the mildest temperatures are \nforecast. If winds go light and the boundary layer decouples, more\neffective radiational cooling could be realized cause \ntemperatures to quickly fall a few more degrees than forecast. \nAfternoon temperatures Saturday only climb to the 60s, maybe \napproaching 70 along the southern coast. Winds more northerly and \ncloser to 10 mph, but could still creep towards 15 mph at times, \nwith gusts to around 20 mph again.\n\nOn Sunday the upper level pattern begins to shift east, sliding \nthe broad mid-upper level low pressure system offshore the \nAtlantic coast, as ridging aloft from the Intermountain West moves \ntowards the Central US. At the surface, high pressure dominates \nthe Eastern US, with the southern flank settling over Florida. \nWinds turn back onshore (NE-ENE), advecting warmer air and \ngradually more moisture from the Atlantic. For most, Sunday \nmorning will be nearly as chilly as Saturday morning with lows in \nthe 40s only a couple degrees warmer and similar wind chills, but\nalong the coast the warming trend will have already started with \nlows in the 50s. Afternoon highs Sunday return to the 70s. Winds \nspeeds again generally around 10 mph with some higher gusts.\n\nMonday-Thursday...Gradual warming and the return of rain chances.\nThe upper air pattern will continue to slowly march east, taking \nthe trough offshore into the Atlantic and bringing the ridge over\nthe eastern half of the CONUS, helping to deepen the surface high\nstalled over the eastern US. The southern flank of the high will \nremain draped across the peninsula, continuing onshore (ENE) flow\nand advecting warm/moist air. The main driver for much of next \nweek's weather will be a lobe or two of vorticity from the western\nUS developing a surface disturbance while undercutting the ridge \nand sliding towards the Southeast and Gulf. There has been better\nagreement between models the last few runs for a wave or weak low\npressure system to move into the Gulf and towards Florida while \nlifting higher moisture, but there is still some uncertainty in \nwhere the system will track and where the highest moisture will \nreach. Also, any southward deviation of the energy aloft towards \nthe front stalled in the southern Gulf could result in a stronger \nlow and greater impacts, like some models were suggesting \nyesterday. For now, onshore flow looks to pick up again with\nincreasing moisture, bringing increasing chances for rain\nespecially along the coast. Will need to monitor the threat for \nbeach/coastal hazards and locally heavy rainfall, but these \nimpacts are not a certainty yet.",
